---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Checked from '../../components/Checked.astro';
import Crumbs from '../../components/Crumbs.astro';
import Newsletter from '../../components/Newsletter.astro';
import { formatDate } from '../../lib/formatDate';
import { handsOnNotes, assertHandsOnCoverage } from '../../lib/handsOnNotes';
import { hubPage } from '../../lib/hubPage';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/withBase';

const entries = await getCollection('use-cases', ({ data }) => !data.draft);
assertHandsOnCoverage(entries);
const titles = new Map(entries.map((entry) => [entry.id, entry.data.title]));
const title = 'Hands-on AI tool tests';
const description = "Dated hands-on notes for the AI tools we actually tried, including Fack'd, AmIPicked and n8n. Standard tasks stay pending.";
const notes = [...handsOnNotes].sort((a, b) => b.date.localeCompare(a.date));
const hub = hubPage(
  Astro.site!,
  Astro.url.pathname,
  title,
  description,
  notes.map((note) => ({ name: note.tool, path: `/use-cases/${note.useCaseSlug}/` })),
);
---
<Base title={title} description={description} jsonLd={hub.jsonLd} hasNewsletter>
  <article class="wrap narrow" data-pagefind-body>
    <section class="hero" style="padding-bottom:8px">
      <Crumbs items={[{ href: withBase('/'), label: 'Home' }, { label: title }]} />
      <span class="eyebrow">Hands-on notes</span>
      <h1>Hands-on AI tool tests</h1>
      <p class="lead">These are the only hands-on notes written on the site. Each one names the date, the tool, and what was actually tried. A shorter check does not turn the use-case page into a completed test.</p>
      <div class="meta"><Checked date={hub.dateModified} label={hub.dateLabel} /></div>
    </section>

    <section class="answer" aria-labelledby="short-answer">
      <h2 id="short-answer">Short answer</h2>
      <p>Nineteen notes exist. Consensus Free on 26 Oct 2026, Perplexity Free on 2 Nov 2026 and Fack'd on 21 Nov 2026 are on the research page. DeepL Free on 3 Nov 2026 is on the translate page. LanguageTool Basic on 4 Nov 2026 and Grammarly Free on 19 Nov 2026 are on the proofread page. Looka Brand Kit on 5 Nov 2026 is on the logo page. Phrasly AI Image Detector on 6 Nov 2026 is on the image-check page. Datastripes on 7 Nov 2026 is on the spreadsheet page. nixm on 8 Nov 2026 is on the writing page. Dessn on 9 Nov 2026 is on the app-screen page. Spacefast on 10 Nov 2026 is on the vibe-code page. Quillo on 11 Nov 2026 is on the voiceover page. Pixelcut and Photoroom, signed out, on 14 Nov 2026 are on the product-photo page. ChatGPT, signed out, on 15 Nov 2026 is on the writing page. Anki desktop on 16 Nov 2026 is on the flashcards page. Zotero desktop on 17 Nov 2026 is on the citation page. n8n self-hosted on 18 Nov 2026 is on the admin page. AmIPicked on 20 Nov 2026 is on the marketing page. No other tool on this site has a hands-on note.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="signup"><Newsletter /></section>

    <section>
      <h2>The notes</h2>
      <div class="verdicts">
        {notes.map((note) => {
          const useCaseTitle = titles.get(note.useCaseSlug);
          if (!useCaseTitle) throw new Error(`Missing title for ${note.useCaseSlug}`);
          return (
            <article class="card">
              <div class="meta" style="margin-top:0">
                <span class="tag tested">Hands-on note <time datetime={note.date}>{formatDate(new Date(`${note.date}T00:00:00.000Z`))}</time></span>
              </div>
              <h3 style="font-size:1.2rem;margin-top:10px">{note.tool}</h3>
              <p class="quotable">{note.quote}</p>
              <details class="hands-on-more">
                <summary>Full note</summary>
                <p>{note.tested}</p>
                <p class="caveat">{note.limits}</p>
              </details>
              <p><a href={withBase(`/use-cases/${note.useCaseSlug}/`)}>{useCaseTitle}</a></p>
            </article>
          );
        })}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="prose">
      <h2>What this page is not</h2>
      <p>The research page, the translate page, the proofread page, the logo page, the image-check page, the spreadsheet page and the writing page stay marked pending. Their standard tasks have not been run. The slide-deck page, the vibe-code page, the app-screen page, the voiceover page, the product-photo page, the flashcards page, the citation page and the admin page stay marked pending. Consensus Pro, Elicit, Gemini Notebook, DeepL Pro Individual, Grammarly Pro, Canva Pro, Google Sheets, Claude, a logged-in ChatGPT Free account, Bolt.new, Lovable, Figma, v0, ElevenLabs, Descript, AnkiWeb, AnkiMobile, Zotero storage, Zapier, n8n Cloud and every pick that is not named above have no hands-on note. None of them has a completed standard-task note. The Looka Brand Kit note is the free logo maker, the editor, the mockup previews and the checkout prices. The paid kit was not bought, no file was downloaded, and a 1080×1080 social graphic was not made. The Phrasly note is five images on the free detector, signed out: two real photos read Likely real, two AI JPEGs were caught by a signature in the file, and the ChatGPT ramen example showed a SynthID watermark and OpenAI's signature. Pixel detection did not run on those AI files. Phrasly's over 99% accuracy figure is the vendor's claim and was not measured. A separate signature-free Midjourney-style file was not tested. The Datastripes note is one signed-out upload of a 20-row CSV. Export and saving need a sign-up. The privacy line on the page, that rows stay in the browser, was not checked by network inspection. The nixm note is one signed-out chat draft on nixm.ai. A still image needed an account and was not generated. No shareable NIXM URL appeared. Magic-link signup, the stated 20-credit grant, video, web research and signed-in stills were not tested. Vendor pages claim logged-out signals are public by link and dissolve after seven days, and some copy says a still can be made without an account. Those claims were not verified. The Dessn note is one Free-plan screen at create.dessn.ai, signed in with an email link and no card. The first frame was about 2 minutes 6 seconds after the prompt. The names, prices, steppers and £10.10 total matched the quantities on screen. Placeholder copy was added, including "Baked this morning on Mill Lane". One step showed "1 action failed" and then the screen rendered. 20 credits were still showing, and Dessn said "This first one is on us", so the credit cost was not confirmed. No export or download control was seen. Figma and v0 were not part of it. The Spacefast note is one anonymous publish, with no account, of an existing two-file HTML page. sf publish . took about 4 seconds. A one-line redeploy took about 3.2 seconds and the change was live on the first check. The plain URL returned HTTP 403, "This space is private", and the page was viewable only through a private link. The anonymous space was set to expire in about 33 hours. Making it public needs a WordPress.com account and was not tested. A plain http:// request returned HTTP 500 instead of redirecting. The pricing figures are from the vendor page. Lovable, Bolt.new and Replit were not part of it. The Quillo note is one signed-out read-aloud on quillo.tr/text-to-speech, with no account and no card. The text was "Hollow Oak Bakery opens at 7:30 on 14/03. Call 020 7946 0018; sourdough is £4.50." The first Listen opened the reader in 1.105 seconds and did not start playback. The reader's Listen was pressed at 10:59:59.412 UTC, and Pause appeared within 0.566 seconds. Sentence 1 was highlighted yellow, and the counter moved from 1/2 to 2/2 by 11:00:09. Audio could not be heard in the test environment, so pronunciation of the time, date, phone number and price is unverified. Playback was confirmed from the player UI only. Signed out, the voices were device voices only: Automatic, Google UK English Female, Google UK English Male and Google US English, with no lock icons. Speeds were 0.8, 0.9, 1, 1.1, 1.25, 1.5, 1.75 and 2x, and 1.25x was selected. A FAQ on the page says device-voice reading has no length limit, and MP3 download is not available on this page. Natural Quillo voices need an account and were not tested. The listen-to-PDF page was opened and not playback-tested. Prices seen on 11 Nov 2026: Free $0; a one-time sign-in trial of 10 cloud minutes and 10 AI pages, no card; Starter $4.99/mo (30 cloud minutes and 15 AI pages a month) or $34.99/yr (20 minutes and 10 pages a month); Pro $9.99/mo (90 minutes and 40 pages a month) or $69.99/yr (60 minutes and 25 pages a month). US prices, and purchases are via mobile app stores. ElevenLabs and Descript were not part of it. The 14 Nov 2026 note is one signed-out check of the free web background removers, in the box browser, with no account, no email, no password, no card and no social sign-in. Optional cookies were rejected. The photo was a Highgrove Royal Garden honey jar, CC BY 4.0, from Wikimedia Commons, resized to 1200×1433. Pixelcut opened the editor at /ai-image-editor?tool=removeBackground. The editor controls appeared 0.85 seconds after upload, and the cut-out was visible within about 1 to 2 seconds. The signed-out download was a PNG at 904×1080, the option labelled Preview resolution 904×1080 Free. The other option was Full resolution 1200×1433 Free with sign in, and that sign-in path was not tested. An upsell, Get Pro, was on the page. Photoroom's free web tool returned a result in a few seconds. A capture 1.5 seconds after upload still showed a loader, and the exact time was not measured. The signed-out download was a PNG at 1200×1433. The white swatch worked signed out. The optional Add background editor asked to log in or sign up and was not pursued. Both kept the gold lid, the amber glass, the honey and the label text (HIGHGROVE, Honey, ROYAL GARDEN, 250g e 8.8oz), and removed the yellow shelf. Neither showed a watermark. Edge quality looked similar at this size, and there was no formal edge-error count. Pixelcut showed a result faster. Photoroom's signed-out file was full resolution, and Pixelcut's was a reduced preview. Photoroom Pro and a Pixelcut Free account were not part of it. The 15 Nov 2026 note is one signed-out check of ChatGPT on chatgpt.com, with no account. Optional cookies were rejected. It is not a logged-in Free account. The Ask ChatGPT box worked without logging in. No model name or message limit was shown. The Hollow Oak Bakery customer email came back in about 7 seconds, from 11:20:23.6 to 11:20:30.7 UTC, as 116 words from the subject through the sign-off, in an Email card with a Subject field and edit and copy buttons. It kept the bakery name, Bath, founded 2026, £18, Saturday 9am–11am collection, no delivery, croissants and cinnamon buns. Sourdough loaves were not mentioned. No awards, street address (only Hollow Oak Bakery, Bath), extra prices, website, extra hours or extra products, and none of the banned words. Added colour only: freshly baked treats, buttery croissants, comforting cinnamon buns. "Filled with some of our favourite... treats" is vague about box contents. After the reply, a Share button, and a banner: "Log in to get answers based on saved chats, plus create images and upload files." A still of a wooden shop sign reading Hollow Oak Bakery was gated within about 8 seconds, with no image. The page said "Image creation is only available when you're logged in. You can keep chatting here, or log in to create images." A logged-in Free account, free message and model limits, file uploads, image quality, and Plus and Pro were not tested. Claude was not part of it. The writing page's standard task has not been run. The 16 Nov 2026 note is one local check of Anki desktop 26.09.4, the official GitHub release for Linux x86_64, with a fresh local profile. No account, no AnkiWeb sign-in, no sync, no add-ons and nothing paid. The download was 194.7 MB compressed, about 665 MB extracted. Launch reached the first-run language dialog in about 5 seconds, with no account or sync prompt. Short Hollow Oak Bakery staff food-hygiene notes were turned into 10 Basic cards, written as a tab-separated text file with Anki header lines, then imported with File > Import. The preview auto-detected the tab separator, the Basic note type and the deck. The result was "10 notes found in file. Of those: 10 new notes imported." in 6.27 seconds. One card added by hand with Add > Basic worked, with no warning. 6 of 6 checked answers matched. For a new card, the buttons showed Again under 1 minute, Hard under 6 minutes, Good under 10 minutes, and Easy 3 to 5 days. The Easy interval varied per card. A card marked Again came back later in the same session. 10 cards were studied in 1.96 minutes (Anki stats, 11.78 seconds per card). Anki has no built-in AI card generation. You write the cards, or use another tool, and import a text file. The download is large. Audio was not tested. AnkiWeb, AnkiMobile and sync were not tested. The flashcards page's standard task has not been run. The citation page stays marked pending. The 17 Nov 2026 note is one local check of Zotero 10.0.8, the Linux x86_64 build from zotero.org, with a fresh local profile. No Zotero account, no sync, no browser connector, no Word or LibreOffice plugin and nothing paid. The download was 118.3 MB, as a .tar.xz file. On first launch Zotero opened its install-confirmation page in the browser and started a LibreOffice citation-plugin installer, which was cancelled. Zotero's own window was usable about 63 seconds after launch, including those two windows. The welcome panel suggested the Quick Start Guide, the Zotero Connector and syncing. No account or payment prompt blocked use. Three sources were added by identifier. DOI 10.1038/nature14539 took about 18 seconds and came back as the journal article Deep learning, by LeCun, Bengio and Hinton, dated 2015-05-28, in Nature, volume 521, pages 436–444. That metadata was correct. ISBN 9780262035613 took about 16 seconds. One earlier attempt did not register because focus moved away. It came back as the book Deep learning, by Goodfellow, Bengio and Courville, 2016, publisher The MIT press, with a lower-case press as returned. It was correct apart from that casing. DOI 10.1145/3442188.3445922 took about 18 seconds and came back as the conference paper On the Dangers of Stochastic Parrots..., by Bender, Gebru, McMillan-Major and Shmitchell, 2021, in the ACM FAccT proceedings, published by ACM, pages 610–623. That metadata was correct. A web page was added by hand: the Food Standards Agency allergen labelling page. The title, website title, URL and accessed date were saved. Create Bibliography from Items, with the style APA Style 7th edition, saved as HTML. There were four entries, alphabetised, with DOIs as links, issue number 7553 on the Nature article, and (n.d.) for the undated web page. The MIT press casing carried through. A collection named Bakery dissertation was created. Dragging items onto it did not add them in this test session. Right-click Add to Collection worked, and both items showed. Settings showed Log In / Create Account. The profile was not signed in. Storage, sync, the browser connector, PDF attachment and citing from Word or LibreOffice were not tested. Zotero storage, the budget pick, was not part of this check. The citation page's standard task has not been run. The admin page stays marked pending. The 18 Nov 2026 note is one local check of n8n 2.48.5, self-hosted Community Edition, installed with npm on Linux x86_64. Node was v24.22.1 and npm was 11.21.1. No n8n account, no n8n Cloud trial, no licence key, no card and no sign-up. It was run with the n8n CLI. The editor UI was not used: on first open it asks for a local owner account, and this unattended run could not do that. npm install took 74.9 seconds. node_modules was 2.9 GB, with 1,096 top-level packages. npm 11 skipped install scripts for 14 packages. n8n still ran. The first command auto-generated an encryption key. Importing the workflow took 5.8 seconds, most of that 344 database migrations, and printed "Successfully imported 1 workflow." The workflow id was HollowOakOrder01. It was a 9-node Hollow Oak Bakery order check, written by us as JSON: flag orders a human needs to look at, and write confirmation emails for the rest, saved as CSV. There were 6 sample orders. The first execute took 3.83 seconds wall clock, exit 0, status success. A second run took 3.51 seconds. The workflow itself ran in 0.80 seconds. Six orders went in, two were flagged and four were confirmed. The CSV files were 313 bytes and 1,442 bytes. Routing was 6 of 6 correct. Totals were 6 of 6 correct: £18.00, £36.00, £8.80 and £18.00 on the confirmations (£80.80 together) and £13.50 and £16.80 on the problem rows. 4 of 4 confirmation bodies included the exact line "Saturday 9–11am collection, Bath, no delivery". n8n executed the workflow. The rules and the email wording were written by us. Nothing was AI-generated. An OpenAI message node with no credential failed with "Node does not have any credentials set". The editor UI, n8n Cloud, AI nodes with a real key, and integrations (Gmail, Google Sheets, Stripe, webhooks, schedules) were not tested. Zapier was not part of this check. The admin page's standard task has not been run. The proofread page stays marked pending. The 19 Nov 2026 note is one signed-out check of Grammarly Free on grammarly.com/grammar-check, at about 11:22 GMT, with no account, in a desktop browser. A short 247-character email with 8 planted errors showed 9 underlines in about 5 seconds. Signed out, only the first suggestion could be read: thank's to thanks, labelled Fix spelling. Hovering or clicking any other underline opened a sign-up modal, "Get Grammarly for free to get more". "Me and the team has" was not flagged. A 618-word blog post, 3,355 characters, was silently cut to 709 characters (about 133 words, about 21%) with no warning, and only 4 suggestions were shown for that part. No rewrite, rephrase or tone option appeared. The page says generative AI, tone and clarity suggestions need an account or Pro. A free Grammarly account signed in, the browser extension, the desktop app, Pro and the paraphrasing tool were not tested. Grammarly Pro was not part of this check. The proofread page's standard task has not been run. The marketing page stays marked pending. The 20 Nov 2026 note is one free check of AmIPicked at amipicked.com, in the box browser, with no sign-up, no email and no card. It is not the standard task. The free check uses ChatGPT and Gemini only. The flow is a URL, a site read of about 4 to 10 seconds, an editable screen titled Here is what we will ask AI, then Ask ChatGPT and Gemini, then a result on the same page. Colonnade Bakery's site did not load (ERR_CONNECTION_CLOSED), so the first check used Hobbs House Bakery, an independent family bakery in the Cotswolds and Bristol, not Bath. A Bath site was not confirmed. That check took about 96 seconds in total and about 75 seconds of asking, and returned 3/9. The page said the business was mentioned in 3 of 5 questions, and AI recommends you only sometimes. Gemini returned This model did not answer. Try again in a minute. on question 1. Hobbs House itself was 33% (avg #1.3). Of two citations opened, one supported the claim and one only partly supported it. The second check was Greggs. It took about 50 seconds in total and about 41 seconds of asking, and returned 8/10. Both engines answered every question. The page said the business was mentioned in 5 of 5 questions, and it still said AI recommends you only sometimes. Greggs itself was 80% (avg #1.8). Of two citations opened, both supported the claim. No checks-left counter was seen. Upsells were Remind me in 30 days, which was not used, a Download PDF button, which was not clicked, and Five questions are a sample. The pricing page lists a free check at $0, Starter at $29/month, Growth at $59/month, and a one-off report at $39. Paid tracking, the PDF download and the email reminder were not tested. The checker reported No llms.txt file and No structured data for greggs.com. That was not checked independently. Buffer Essentials and Metricool Free were not part of this check. The marketing page's standard task has not been run. The research page stays marked pending. The 21 Nov 2026 note is one free check of Fack'd at fackd.com, signed in with an email code and no password. It checks claims in a YouTube video in real time, from the captions. It is not the standard task. Signed out, the homepage was DOM-ready in 0.5 seconds and load was 0.7 seconds. An already-checked NYT video showed 3 claims. One opened source supported a Supported rating. A Wikipedia opening section only weakly or partly supported a Misleading rating. It was unclear whether the signed-out list is capped. The free check used a 6-minute TalkTV debate that was not in the library. Searches for a Nov 2026 Kuenssberg or budget interview found nothing. The check started at about 11:18:20. The first claim appeared at about 11:19:25, at 0:38. The result was 3 claims, 2 Supported and 1 Missing context, and none were Misleading or False. Two opened sources supported the ratings. Speaker labels named the host, Jeremy Kyle, even when a panellist spoke. The counter started at 10 free checks left and fell to 4. It dropped about one per minute of video, from 10 to 9, then 8, then 7, then 5, and autoplay kept checking a Daily Show clip until Stop checking was clicked, so the 6-minute video plus the overrun cost 6 of 10. Nothing was visibly locked. A Go Premium button was the only upsell. There were no errors. Videos longer than about 10 minutes, the Chrome extension, live streams and Premium were not tested. The pricing page lists Free at $0, Premium at $12/mo and Pro at $49/mo. Consensus Pro and Elicit Basic were not part of this check. The research page's standard task has not been run.</p>
      <p>Prices elsewhere on the site are vendor figures with a source and a checked date. They are not measurements from these notes.</p>
      <p>Browse <a href={withBase('/use-cases/')}>all use cases</a>, <a href={withBase('/best/')}>all shortlists</a> and <a href={withBase('/about/')}>how we test</a>.</p>
    </section>

  </article>
</Base>
